sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2024-collective-09> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Bioelectric and biochemical perturbations of vertebrate left-right patterning randomize laterality only at the level of whole embryos (each embryo's cells agree on L and R identity); the only known perturbation that breaks this concordance is down-regulation of planar-polarity VANGL signalling, which produces speckled L/R marker expression on both sides of the midline.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Left-right randomization is concordant within an embryo except after VANGL knockdown";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "McMillen P, Levin M, “Collective intelligence: A unifying concept for integrating biology across scales and substrates”, Communications biology (2024), doi:10.1038/s42003-024-06037-4; section “Introduction › Left/right, head/tail: random, but collective anatomical decisions”. Scope: Vertebrate embryos (chick, Xenopus and others) with perturbed left-right pathways"
}
